.TH NOSCAT 1 "2026" "noscat 0.5.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
noscat \- like netcat, but over your own device mesh: npub-addressed, end-to-end encrypted, no root, no accounts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B noscat
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.I COMMAND
.RI [ ARGS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B noscat
joins your devices into a private mesh. Each device has its own
self-generated Nostr identity (a keypair: no email, no phone number, no
account) and is addressed by its npub. Membership is a Marmot group (MLS)
carried by Nostr relays; every member can verify who is in the mesh and
any member can manage it. Data goes device to device over FIPS, an
end-to-end encrypted overlay keyed by the same identities, with QUIC
streams on top. Relays never see who is in a mesh, device names,
addresses or data. Only members can reach a device, and a device can only
reach members.
.PP
The protocol is specified in
.I docs/nip-noscat.md
in the repository. noscat 0.5 does not talk to 0.4 and reads none of its
state.
.SH THE DEVICE
Each machine is one device. Its state (identity and mesh) lives in the
platform data directory:
.I ~/.local/share/noscat
on Linux and the BSDs (or
.IR $XDG_DATA_HOME/noscat ),
.I ~/Library/Application Support/noscat
on macOS,
.I %APPDATA%\(rsnoscat
on Windows. The identity is
.I identity.nsec
there, created (mode 0600) on first use.
.PP
Only one process runs a device. While
.B noscat up
(or
.BR expose )
runs, every other
.B noscat
command on the same directory goes through it; when none runs, a command
starts the device just for itself and stops it when done.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B id
Print this device's npub and its pairing QR (which encodes
.IR noscat:<npub> ).
.TP
.B keygen
Generate a throwaway identity and print its npub. With
.B \-\-show\-secret
also print the secret key (hex). Devices create their own identity on
first use, so this is rarely needed.
.TP
.B up
Run this device until Ctrl-C: receive files, chat, and serve members.
Prints the pairing QR and runs a terminal chat (see
.BR CHAT ).
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-out <dir>
Where received files go (default
.IR ~/Downloads ).
.TP
.B \-\-to <addr>
Bridge members' default-service streams (see
.BR PORTS )
to this TCP service, e.g. 127.0.0.1:22.
.TP
.B \-\-serve\-port <port>
Let members forward to this local port by number (repeatable).
.TP
.B \-\-accept\-joins
Invite every device that sends a join request, without asking. A join
request only needs this device's npub, which is not secret, so use it
while setting devices up and turn it off afterwards.
.RE
.TP
.BI "expose \-\-to " addr
Run this device and expose one TCP service to members as its default
service.
.TP
.B serve
Netcat listen: bridge the first member stream to stdio.
.TP
.BI pipe " target"
Netcat connect: bridge stdio to a member's default service.
.TP
.BI send " path target"
Send a file to a member. Transfers resume: an interrupted send leaves a
partial file on the receiver, and sending the same file again continues
where it stopped. The receiver verifies the whole file against the
sender's sha256 before renaming it into place.
.TP
.BI chat " target text..."
Send a chat message to a member; exits once it is delivered.
.TP
.BI clip " target"
Share this machine's clipboard with a member; it becomes their
clipboard.
.TP
.BI "forward " target " \-\-port " port
Listen on local TCP
.I port
and carry each connection to a member's default service, or with
.B \-\-remote\-port <port>
to a port the member serves
.RB ( "up \-\-serve\-port" ).
The listener binds 127.0.0.1;
.B \-\-bind <ip>
exposes it further, and anyone who can reach it can use the forward.
.TP
.B name \fR[\fInew name\fR]
Print this device's name, or rename it. The name is saved for every later
run, and a running device tells the members right away.
.B \-\-name
overrides it for one run.
.TP
.B service install \fR[\fIup options\fR]
Run
.B noscat up
in the background at login, with the given
.B up
options and global options (such as
.BR \-\-reachable ).
On Linux it writes the systemd user unit
.IR ~/.config/systemd/user/noscat.service ,
starts it, and enables lingering so it also runs at boot and after
logout; logs:
.BR "journalctl \-\-user \-u noscat" .
On macOS it installs a launchd agent logging to
.IR ~/Library/Logs/noscat.log .
Installing again replaces it.
.TP
.B service uninstall
Stop and remove it.
.TP
.B service status
Whether it runs, with its latest log lines.
.TP
.BI completions " shell"
Print shell completions (bash, elvish, fish, powershell, zsh).
.PP
A
.I target
is a member's npub, or the
.I noscat:<npub>
text of its QR.
.SH MESHES
A mesh is a Marmot (MLS) group. Every member is an admin: any member can
invite and remove, and losing any one device loses nothing.
.TP
.BI "mesh create " name
Start a new mesh with this device as its only member.
.TP
.BI "mesh join " inviter
Ask the member
.I inviter
(the device whose QR you scanned) to invite this device, wait for the
invitation and join. Only that member's invitation is accepted.
.B \-\-timeout <secs>
gives up (default 600).
.TP
.BI "mesh invite " target
Add a device. It must be running, or waiting in
.BR "mesh join" .
A running
.B up
prints each join request with the
.B invite
line to type.
.TP
.BI "mesh remove " target
Remove a member. Each member cuts it off as soon as it processes the
removal.
.TP
.B mesh leave
Leave the mesh. Another member completes it;
.B \-\-timeout <secs>
waits for that (default 120). The last member just forgets the mesh.
.TP
.B mesh requests
List the devices that asked to join (scanned this device's QR) in the
last 15 minutes and are not members yet. In a terminal, ask y/n for each
and invite it on y. Works next to a running service: requests are read
from the relays.
.TP
.B mesh members
List the members, their names, presence, which are connected (data can
flow to them now; only known while
.B up
runs) and which are reachable, with a note when none is. A member can be
online (its advert arrived over the relays) without being connected.
.TP
.B mesh qr
Print this device's pairing QR. In a private mesh it also names the
mesh's relays
.RB ( noscat:<npub>?relay=... ),
so a device that scans it joins through them alone.
.TP
.B mesh relays \fR[\fBadd\fR|\fBremove\fR \fIurl\fR...]
List the mesh's relays and whether it is private, or add and remove
some. Any member can change them; every member moves to the new list.
.TP
.B mesh private
Make the mesh private: every device uses only the mesh's relays, logs in
to them with NIP-42 as its device key, and stops using its own and the
public ones. For a members-only relay such as noscat-relay; add it with
.B mesh relays add
first. noscat never logs in to any other relay.
.TP
.B mesh shared
Make the mesh shared again: devices use their own relays too, and log in
nowhere.
.PP
Every mesh needs at least one member the others can reach from the
internet: a home server with a forwarded UDP port, a VPS, or anything with
a public IPv6 address. Run it with
.BR \-\-reachable .
Members behind NAT reach each other directly where they can and through
that member otherwise. Members on the same LAN find each other by mDNS.
.SH PORTS
A member's
.I default service
is whatever it exposes
.RB ( "up \-\-to"
or
.BR expose ).
Other local ports are reachable only if the member serves them by number
.RB ( "up \-\-serve\-port" ).
.SH CHAT
.B up
runs a terminal chat on stdin. Incoming messages print with the
sender's name.
Type
.B to
to list the members and
.BI to " <name or npub prefix>"
to pick one; every following line goes to them.
.B clip
shares the local clipboard with the selected member,
.BI name " <new name>"
renames this device (saved, like
.BR "noscat name" ),
and
.BI invite " <npub>"
adds a device that asked to join.
.SH OPTIONS
These apply to every command.
.TP
.B \-\-dir <dir>
The device state directory (default: see
.BR "THE DEVICE" ).
.TP
.B \-\-relay <url>
A relay to use (repeatable). Default: relay.snort.social, relay.ditto.pub
and nostr.mom. A mesh also keeps the relays it was created with.
.TP
.B \-\-listen <addr>
UDP address of the data plane. Default: any free port, 41641 with
.BR \-\-reachable .
.TP
.B \-\-name <name>
The name other members see, for this run. Default: the name saved by
.BR "noscat name" ,
else the hostname.
.TP
.B \-\-reachable [<addr>]
This device is reachable from the internet. Give its public address, or
nothing to learn it by STUN. Forward UDP 41641 to it, or set
.BR \-\-listen .
.TP
.B \-\-peer <npub@ip:port>
A known address of a device (repeatable).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-lan
Do not look for members on the LAN (mDNS).
.TP
.B \-\-verbose
Info-level logs, plus the FIPS warnings hidden by default because they are
routine: a capped UDP receive buffer, a member with no address yet, a
direct path that could not be set up or went stale (traffic keeps flowing
through another member), and congestion marks during a busy transfer. Other warnings always print;
.B RUST_LOG
overrides.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NOSCAT_DIR
The device state directory, like
.BR \-\-dir .
.TP
.B NOSCAT_IDENTITY
The identity (hex or nsec) instead of
.IR identity.nsec .
It is read from the environment so it never appears in the process list.
.TP
.B RUST_LOG
Log filter, e.g.
.BR noscat=debug .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success (for
.BR send :
the whole file was received and verified; for
.BR chat :
the member acknowledged it).
.TP
.B nonzero
Failure: not a member, no route, timeout, a refused transfer, or the
device is busy in another process.
.SH EXAMPLES
A first device, reachable from the internet:
.PP
.nf
    noscat mesh create home
    noscat up \-\-reachable
.fi
.PP
A second device (scan or copy the first one's npub), then approve on the
first with the printed
.B invite
line:
.PP
.nf
    noscat mesh join npub1...
.fi
.PP
SSH to a member through the mesh:
.PP
.nf
    noscat up \-\-to 127.0.0.1:22          # on the server
    noscat forward npub1... \-\-port 2222   # on the laptop
    ssh \-p 2222 localhost
.fi
.PP
Netcat:
.PP
.nf
    noscat serve                       # on A
    echo hello | noscat pipe npub1...  # on B, A's npub
.fi
